When Ingrid Halvorsen set out to ski to the South Pole, she had spent four years and most of her savings preparing for it. She and her two companions would haul sledges weighing ninety kilos each across eleven hundred kilometres of ice, with no resupply and no support team. Nine weeks later, a hundred and thirty kilometres short of their goal, they radioed for a plane.

{{41}}

Trouble had begun long before that. In the third week, a storm pinned the team down for six days, and the schedule they had spent years perfecting was out of date before they had even reached the plateau.

{{42}}

Halvorsen did the sums every evening, scratching figures into a notebook with gloved fingers. By week six, the numbers told a story she did not want to hear: at their current pace, they would reach the Pole with two days’ food left and no margin for error.

{{43}}

He was the last to admit how bad it was, and by the time he did, the team was already ten days behind. Until then he had refused to let anyone else take his turn at the front, insisting that the hands would recover on their own.

{{44}}

Her teammates did not argue. The plane that came for them took four hours to arrive, and in that time hardly anyone spoke. They sat on their sledges, facing south.

{{45}}

When she finally read them, they were not what she had feared. Most came from strangers, many of them people who had themselves turned back from something: a mountain, a marathon, a failing business. One, from a retired polar guide, simply said that the bravest decision on any expedition is the one that nobody applauds.

{{46}}
